--TEST--
The sealed door is the only door: `get` answers `null` for a name `putSecret` wrote, a plain `put` under that name is a separate entry, and `forget` takes the plain one and leaves the sealed one — `rule:security/secret-crosses-no-boundary`, `rule:concurrency/cross-request-state-is-explicit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `put` refuses a `secret` at the boundary and `getSecret` is where one goes
// instead. The other half of that is asserted here: nothing a `putSecret` wrote
// is reachable through the members that copy a value in and out, so a program
// cannot arrive at the ciphertext by asking the ordinary way.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];

secret string $token = "hunter2";

var $local = Core\Cache::local();
var $process = Core\Cache::process();

$local->putSecret("token", $token, 1h, $ring);
$process->putSecret("token", $token, 1h, $ring);

// Counted over both tiers, because a tier that handed back the sealed payload
// and a tier that answered the miss print the same shape of row on their own.
int $blind = 0;
if ($local->get("token") == null) {
    $blind = $blind + 1;
}
if ($process->get("token") == null) {
    $blind = $blind + 1;
}
echo "get is blind to a sealed entry: ", $blind, " of 2", "\n";

// The two live in key spaces of their own, so writing the ordinary way under
// the same name neither replaces the sealed entry nor is replaced by it.
$local->put("token", "plain");
echo "a plain put under the same name: ", $local->get("token") as string, "\n";
echo "and the sealed entry is untouched: ",
    ($local->getSecret("token", $ring) ?? "") == $token ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// The same claim through the third member: forgetting the name takes the entry
// `put` wrote and leaves the one `putSecret` wrote.
$local->forget("token");
echo "forget takes the plain entry: ", $local->get("token") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";
echo "and leaves the sealed one: ",
    ($local->getSecret("token", $ring) ?? "") == $token ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
--EXPECT--
get is blind to a sealed entry: 2 of 2
a plain put under the same name: plain
and the sealed entry is untouched: yes
forget takes the plain entry: null
and leaves the sealed one: yes
